# A MODULE-QUALIFIED call must reach the name the source wrote, and Python names
# are CASE-SENSITIVE.
#
# `casecls.alpha([1, 2])` was matched against `class Alpha` -- case-insensitively,
# Pascal-style -- and compiled as a CONSTRUCTION. The function never ran, its
# argument arrived as the constructor's `key`, and the value that came back was a
# plausible Alpha instead of the one the factory builds. Silent.
#
# THE ARM THAT DECIDES IT IS `PyQualCtorSegs`, AND IT HAS TWO DISJUNCTS. The
# second asks PyIsExactCtorName and is exact. The first was added later so a
# NilPy module's keyword-named `class set` could be constructed through a
# qualifier, was deliberately narrowed to the qualifier's own unit, and did not
# carry the exactness test across -- so the hole this family had already been
# closed at reopened one disjunct over.
# bug-n-a-qualified-module-member-is-taken-by-a-case-folded-local-class is the
# same rule at the arm's OUTER guard; this is the inner one.
#
# `class Menu` beside `def menu(...)` is the shape, and it is how a Python module
# is written rather than a corner: lekkerzeilen/ui.py has exactly that pair, and
# `ui.menu(self.panels)` built a Menu whose `self` was a code address, so the
# demo SEGFAULTED nine seconds in with a rip inside the constructor.
#
# BOTH DECLARATION ORDERS are here, and the class-last row is the one a suite
# written the ordinary way would omit. Both were measured wrong before the fix,
# so this arm is not order-sensitive -- which is itself worth pinning, because
# the cheap reading of "the class wins" is that it registered first.
import casecls

print("1", casecls.alpha([1, 2]).what)
print("2", casecls.Alpha("k").what)
print("3", casecls.beta([1, 2]).what)
print("4", casecls.Beta("k").what)

# A function-against-function and a global-against-global collision, plus a
# global against a class: all correct before the fix. Without them a repaired
# row cannot be told from a lookup that simply stopped folding case anywhere.
print("5", casecls.eps())
print("6", casecls.EPS())
print("7", casecls.DELTA)
print("8", casecls.Delta().what)
print("9", casecls.zeta)
print("10", casecls.ZETA)

# The case the broken disjunct was written FOR. A keyword-named class, spelled
# plainly, constructed through the qualifier -- it must keep working, and it is
# why the repair narrows the match rather than deleting the arm.
print("11", casecls.set(3).n)
print("12", casecls.setval(1))

# UNQUALIFIED, in the program's own scope: correct before the fix, because the
# bare-name intercept one screen up has asked the exact predicate since the
# first member of this family. Here so the fixture says WHERE the defect is.
class Gamma:
    def __init__(self, key):
        self.what = "Gamma.__init__ key=" + str(key)


def gamma(x):
    return Gamma("made-by-gamma")


print("13", gamma([1, 2]).what)
print("14", Gamma("k").what)
